@@ -589,6 +589,12 @@ The bot's own ID is always excluded (infinite-loop prevention). Messages from al
 
 Consecutive replies to the same bot are capped at `RESPOND_TO_BOTS_MAX_CONSECUTIVE` (default 3). The counter resets when a human or a different bot posts. This is a safety net against runaway bot-to-bot loops.
 
+For bot messages, only a **real mention in the message body** (`<@BotID>`) keeps the conversation going. A Discord reply carries a mention of the replied-to user (`replied_user`) even without one in the body; for bots that signal is ignored on both the receiving and the sending side, so a reply saying "let's stop here" no longer re-ignites the exchange. The reply form itself stays usable.
+
+Human messages are unchanged: a plain reply to the bot still triggers it, and xangi's replies still ping you (`replied_user`). `repliedUser: false` applies **only when replying to a bot**. Body mentions are resolved with `parse: ['users']`; `@everyone` / `@here` / role mentions are never resolved.
+
+For bot messages the check also looks at the **raw body text**, not just the `message.mentions` Discord resolved. If the peer bot's send path has no mention-resolution option, `<@BotID>` in the body arrives with an empty `message.mentions` and the conversation silently stalls (the client still renders it as a mention pill, which makes it hard to spot). This text fallback applies to bot messages only, and it preserves both invariants: a bare reply never wakes the bot, and dropping the mention always stops the exchange. Side effect: **a bot quoting another bot's ID in `<@...>` form will wake that bot**, so avoid that form when discussing IDs.
+
 `/respondtobots` toggles the feature ON/OFF dynamically and persists to `.env`. To disable this command, set `ALLOW_RESPOND_TO_BOTS_COMMAND=false` in `.env` (default: enabled).
 
 Use case: run multiple xangi instances (e.g. xangi-prod=Claude / xangi-dev=Local LLM) in the same channel and compare their responses to the same prompt side-by-side.
